@page "/sourcery/simd-part-two-intrinsics-and-you"
@using Sorcery.Shared.Components.Blogging;
@using Sorcery.Blogging;
@using Sorcery.Shared.Components.Footnotes
@inject BlogBook BlogBook;

@code {
    public static readonly RenderFragment Introduction = __builder =>
    {
        <Paragraph>
            Compiler designers put abstraction layers making SIMD portable and more palatable to the average programmer,
            but underneath there lurk the basic building blocks of raw CPU instructions. Instead of relying on the compiler
            to emit proper opcodes for the host platform, we can use the power of <Highlight>intrinsics</Highlight>
            to build up SIMD programs ourselves.
        </Paragraph>
    };
}

<BlogPost Post="BlogBook.SimdPartTwoIntrinsicsAndYou">
    @Introduction

    <MudAlert Variant="Variant.Outlined" Class="mb-4">
        This is Part 2 of the SIMD introductory series and might make no sense for you without the context of the
        previous post below.
    
        <BlogPostCard Post="@BlogBook.SimdCheatCodesForFreePerformance" class="my-2"/>
    </MudAlert>
    
    <Paragraph>
        While a portable SIMD abstraction is an extremely useful tool, as is common for the craft of sourcery,
        it is not enough to just know the surface. <Footnote>
            Indeed, <Link Href="https://www.joelonsoftware.com/2002/11/11/the-law-of-leaky-abstractions/">
                all abstractions leak
            </Link> and you can only fix the leak if you understand what's being abstracted away.
        </Footnote>
        We need to know what work the JIT is actually doing and how to check
        that work if something goes awry, for example the performance of our vectorised code is not as we expected.
        Fortunately, with intrinsics I believe I can explain it to you without actually showing scary assembly code.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Paragraph>
        I'll first tell you what intrinsics are and give you a glimpse at the madness of all the different SIMD instruction
        sets, then we'll apply this by reimplementing last post's Discrepancy algorithm using intrinsics, and finally
        we'll see that we can use it to directly access operations that are not exposed in the portable <Code>VectorN</Code>
        API.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Header2>Intrinsics</Header2>
    
    <Paragraph>
        A <Highlight>compiler intrinsic</Highlight> is a function in a high-level programming language that is not
        actually a function implemented in that language, rather a special codeword for the compiler to emit a specific
        low-level operation; this can be a single instruction or a sequence of multiple instructions, depending on the
        intrinsic. They are not a SIMD-specific concept &ndash; indeed, two most popular intrinsics on x86 are bitwise
        operations on a regular register, <Code>lzcnt</Code> and <Code>popcnt</Code>.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Paragraph>
        Leading zero count, <Code>lzcnt</Code>, is a hardware instruction available on x86 with the BMI instruction
        set extension. It returns the number of zeroes in a given machine word counting from "the left", or from
        the most significant bit (x86 is little-endian). For example, on x86-64:
        <MudList Dense="true">
            <MudListItem><Code>lzcnt 0</Code> is $64$;</MudListItem>
            <MudListItem><Code>lzcnt 1</Code> is $63$;</MudListItem>
            <MudListItem><Code>lzcnt 64</Code> ($2^{6}$) is $57$;</MudListItem>
            <MudListItem><Code>lzcnt 2147483648</Code> ($2^{32}$) is $32$;</MudListItem>
            <MudListItem><Code>lzcnt 9223372036854775808</Code> ($2^{63}$) is $0$.</MudListItem>
        </MudList>
        This can be computed "by hand" by computing $\log_2$ with some fancy bit-tricks,<Footnote>
            <Link Href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/11398748/4646738">See this DeBruijn algorithm.</Link>
        </Footnote> but the dedicated <Code>lzcnt</Code> instruction is faster.
        The <Link Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.numerics.bitoperations.leadingzerocount"><Code>BitOperations.LeadingZeroCount</Code></Link>
        is a portable function &ndash; on a platform that supports <Code>lzcnt</Code> it will use that, falling
        back to a manual implementation otherwise.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Paragraph>
        <Link Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.runtime.intrinsics.x86.lzcnt.leadingzerocount?view=net-8.0#system-runtime-intrinsics-x86-lzcnt-leadingzerocount(system-uint32)">
            <Code>System.Runtime.Intrinsics.X86.Lzcnt.LeadingZeroCount</Code>
        </Link> is an <em>intrinsic</em>. It doesn't actually have a body, it just tells the compiler to always emit
        the <Code>lzcnt</Code> instruction in its place. This gives us direct control over the emitted instructions,
        but doesn't throw us directly into assembly land &ndash; from the programmer's perspective it acts just like
        a regular .NET method, taking a regular <Code>uint</Code> and giving a regular <Code>uint</Code> in return.
        However, it also requires us to be certain that the instruction set actually supports what we want to emit.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Header2>Instruction Sets and Extensions</Header2>
    
    <Paragraph>
        An <Highlight>instruction set</Highlight> defines, among others, registers and instructions that have
        to be available on a CPU implementing it. Different architectures, x86, x86-64 ARM, RISC-V, or even WebAssembly
        in a browser, have completely different instructions even for the basic operations,
        like adding together two integers; naturally, their
        SIMD operations are also different, and often fundamentally irreconcilable. <Footnote>
            For example, as of this writing ARM has no counterpart for the 
            <Link Href="https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/docs/intrinsics-guide/index.html#text=_mm256_mask_compress_epi8&ig_expand=1319">
                <Code>compresstore</Code> instruction
            </Link>
            and it's basically impossible to emulate it with simpler operations.
        </Footnote>
        The x86-64 instruction by itself doesn't define SIMD operations, neither does ARM or wasm,
        but there are multiple <Highlight>extensions</Highlight> that a particular implementation can include.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Paragraph>
        From now on let's focus on x86, since it's frankly the most complicated zoo of extensions.
        The most basic one is SSE (Streaming SIMD Extensions) and SSE2.
        <Footnote>
            SSE only defined SIMD for 32-bit floating point numbers, while SSE2 introduced 64-bit floats and 8- through 64-bit integers.
        </Footnote>
        They define 128-bit wide SIMD vectors and operations on them. Later these were extended by SSE3, SSSE3, and SSE4.
        Next up came AVX (Advanced Vector Extensions), and AVX2 which introduced 256-bit registers. The hottest and
        newest is AVX512, which provides 512-bit registers and fun masking operations; however, this is not an extension
        in itself, instead it's a whole family of separate extensions starting from AVX512-F, through
        AVX512-{CD, ER, PF, VL, DQ, BW, IFMA, VBMI, 4VNNIW, 4FMAPS, VPOPCNTDQ,
        VKITCHENSINK, VNNI, VBMI2, BITALG, VP2INTERSECT, GFNI, VPCLMULQDQ, VAES}.
        <Footnote>
            I can't stress how funny it is that CLMUL is still a super special little boy that needs its own extension
            flag. It's a fundamentally simpler operation than a full MUL, which doesn't need any special flags!
        </Footnote>
        Only one of those I just made up. Needless to say, it's a mess.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Paragraph>
        The relations between those are not obvious. For example, every CPU that implements SSSE3 must also implement
        SSE3, which in turn guarantees SSE2;
        every CPU that supports AVX2 supports AVX and SSE4; <em>but</em> a CPU can support AVX and not SSE3. For a more
        horrific example, CLMUL is a separate extension that introduces one instruction, <Code>pclmulqdq</Code>, and is
        guaranteed by AVX2, but not by any earlier extension. This doesn't mean it <em>can't</em> be present &ndash;
        a CPU with SSSE3 and CLMUL but not AVX2 can exist! And figuring out which individual morsels of AVX512 are
        enabled where <Link Href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Advanced_Vector_Extensions#CPUs_with_AVX-512">is a nightmare</Link>.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Paragraph>
        When playing with <Code>Vector128</Code>/<Code>Vector256</Code> from the previous post we don't have to worry
        about any of this. The compiler will automatically use the best instructions it can. If we use
        <Code>Vector256</Code> on a platform that only has SSE2, the JIT will emulate 256-bit wide instructions by
        using twice as many 128-bit instructions. It can also automatically figure out that some of the operations
        defined on <Code>Vector128</Code> can be executed more efficiently using operations from AVX512-VL, which
        allows masking instructions from AVX512 to operate on registers from SSE2, if the host platform supports
        that extension.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Paragraph>
        When going to the level of intrinsics we <em>absolutely</em> need to worry about this, because the compiler will
        dutifully emit exactly what we tell it to, and if that code then executes on an unsupported platform the sky
        will fall &ndash; most likely the CPU won't understand the opcode and a SIGILL will kill the process.
        <Footnote>
            Funnily enough, this is arguably relatively benign compared to UB that could corrupt your memory or lead to
            invalid logic being executed.
        </Footnote>
    </Paragraph>
    
    <Header2>Back to Discrepancy</Header2>
    
    <Paragraph>
        As an exercise, let us reimplement the exact same algorithm as last time, but specifically for x86-64 AVX2
        instructions using intrinsics. We will be able to verify that we get the exact same performance.
    </Paragraph>
    
    <MudAlert Variant="Variant.Outlined" Class="mb-4">
        We will discuss juicy details in the next part. To not miss it, subscribe to the <Link Href="https://v0ldek.com/feed.rss">RSS feed of Sourcery</Link>,
        or watch the Discussions in <Link Href="@GitHub.Sorcery">the GitHub repo</Link>!
    </MudAlert>
</BlogPost>
